[Music]  lisa-marie finally opened the door the  fifth time she tried it was about  midnight her twins and her  fifteen-year-old were in her rusted  green Honda she drove to the yellow  brick house that she had found with a  unitedway hand on it the local shelter  knocked on the door she was safe for six  months they got her into safe housing at  a local co-op she thought it would be  great her twins were freaked out of  their minds her teenage daughter didn't  leave the room for six months she was  finally invited by some of the residents  to a basement where they were writing a  proposal for $8,000 to United Way they  thought after years of agencies trying  that they were going to be able to help  young people get to school ready do  better in their EQA Oh scores help high  school dropout rates and stop the amount  of times that the cops were coming into  the neighborhood and this group of four  women not only applied got the funding  from United Way but they started a local  childcare co-op they got Lisa Marie's  daughter out of her bedroom to start an  after-school program with 10 20 40 kids  they started community gardens and one  summer afternoon Lisa Marie got up to  receive the Canadian co-op  Federation award for the best co-op in  Canada and her first words were we are  who we are with and where we are with  them it's good to be with you here at  the YW a United Way partner on these  hallowed lands from the indigenous  community because you really are our  partners but this story actually goes  back a lot longer a priest a rabbi two  ministers and a woman advocate this is  not a joke I'm not doing got together in  this bar there was all kinds of problems  in this community their jobs were drying  up  marriages were breaking up people were  fighting people were drinking they said  we've got to find a way to solve some of  these community issues and in 1887 in  Denver these five came up with the first  United Way ever and they went around  with envelopes and their hands out  raising money and since then United Way  has been following that damn thermometer  and then about a hundred years after  that first meeting in the bar a United  Way in the u.s. said that's it  it's about community and every United  Way across the world started digging  back into community here in Toronto and  York Region the largest United Way in  the world we turned outward and we  started talking to residents community  groups about issues income gap early  learning mental health lack of social  housing you know all of the mallet  and what became very clear community is  the solution and we are not going to  fundraise social service or charity our  way out of this stuff if that's all you  remember about me today I hope you  remember that we are who we are with and  where we are with them community is the  answer and we are not going to fundraise  social service or charity our way out of  these social issues and so we in United  Way shifted from the thermometer to the  community and at the core of that shift  has been our research capacity our  commitment to community research in all  of its complexity some reports some  town-hall discussions and merging that  together to shape our work together as  we go forward  good old community work no drive through  accelerated solution no single bullet no  one agency no program that we're gonna  scale no one population that we're gonna  focus on community for slow lasting  measurable change and these six pillars  research first and then investment  evaluation strategy partnerships and  advocacy and research has always helped  us identify what the pressures were  across this region some of you will know  our poverty by postal code report which  transformed how we do business and where  we invest in the community  research allows us to build partnerships  and uprisings of care with others when  we released our precarious employment  report pep so it was a partnership with  McMaster Labor Ministry of Labour the  Toronto Star and most recently with KPMG  where we are using precarity as a tool  to engage with corporate partners and  shift their HR policies research forms  the basis of our public policy work  whether it's the vulnerable workers Act  that was passed unanimously at Queen's  Park or our recent work around community  benefits agreement we're in partnership  with Metrolinx and the province we are  ensuring that every Metrolinx project  from the Eglinton crosstown right across  to all of their builds will ensure 10%  of the workers come from that local  community that is policy and impact on  the ground and research has identified  how we invest in the community when you  think about poverty by postal code not  only did it shape our building strong  neighborhood strategy with the city but  it also led to priority neighborhoods  seven hubs that have been built and the  complete re-energizing of our agency  funding a few thoughts for you around  how we measure these results like so  many of you we measure the effectiveness  of our investment in a number of ways  and behind the scenes we fight like hell  on what is an outcome an output an input  we measure the number of people served  by our agencies the number of visits to  the hubs which equals now about 1.5  million people who have met at the hub  met other people participated in the hub  strata  we monitor the organizational health of  a fragile expansive network of agencies  that exist across the region but because  we are a learning organization we have  also taken our research into community  whether it's at our action for  neighborhood change partnerships with  residents across the region or a series  of meeting house conversations right  across this region church halls  basements where we have brought  residents together to talk about the key  issues in all of them what's become  clear and what resonates today is that  if we really want to change the  continuum for supports and and move the  needle for children we have got to move  the needle for children families  neighborhoods and we cannot do that  without looking at community in its  entirety we also know that our research  public policy agenda and government  relations are our single best strategy  to move government on large scale like  in the community benefits agreement to  make change so a quick example on how  research has influenced our work on the  ground the poverty by postal code report  released in 2004 examined the increase  between 1981 and 2001 in the number of  high poverty neighborhoods across  Toronto and how that concentration of  poverty was impacting vulnerable groups  not only were we able to work with the  then government of Ontario on their  poverty reduction strategy but early on  we completely shifted our funding from  the inner core of Toronto where the bulk  of our agencies were into what is today  a map of our funding across the region  which is a massive you right across the  suburbs interestingly about a decade  later we are not having to re-examine  if we need to put more services back  into the core of Toronto as it  intensifies and changes but thus the  value of our partnership and our  continued commitment we came together  with the City of Toronto to look at the  building strong neighbourhoods strategy  and as I mentioned earlier built seven  hubs with an additional hub underway  this report then led to vertical poverty  in 2011 which examined the growing  concentration of poverty in many of our  high-rise towers and in 2012 we launched  the tower neighborhood renewal  initiative which brings together  stakeholders to revitalize tower  communities the City of Toronto has  since reformed zoning bylaws to promote  neighborhood vibrancy and allow for  programming so that there can be  economic vitality in those neighborhoods  the last piece I want to talk to you  about in terms of our research a  cumulative of poverty by postal code -  the opportunity equation which positions  Toronto as the income disparity capital  of Canada right through to our recent  work around youth unemployment all of  that and our work on the ground in  neighborhoods is transforming how we  invest with our agencies currently over  five million dollars of our investment  goes directly to children what I'd like  to speak to you more about is the total  more holistic investment about two weeks  ago we announced a record eighty seven  million dollar commitment to programs  and agencies across the region it's a  record of investment about a million  dollars more than last year what's  interesting about this based on all of  our research is we are deepening our  work in poverty and geography  number one we have invested in 62 anchor  agency partners they will work with us  for five years and an up and down  strategy frontline support on the ground  community development in their  neighborhood and public policy and  advocacy and research as well because we  know that we're not in a social service  and charity our way out of this and we  need this inner core of 62 partners  across this region deep in poverty work  to to turn the needle on some of these  pressing social issues in addition we  are for the first time in a decade  opening funding for new programming so  that our current agencies and new  partners can apply under 9 program  streams from early years through to  mental health through the homelessness  for us to continue to meet the needs of  this growing region we have to maintain  work with our current partners but open  it up for new charities and supporters  as well you would know in all of your  work that for us to continue to make an  impact and turn the needle not only do  we have to have strong research but we  have to ensure that that investment is  following the data that summer day at  that podium receiving the co-op award  lisa-marie said when I first got here  nobody came out of their houses today 85  percent of our community is connected  and I feel like I belong belong it's the  most powerful word I'm involved in this  community I've got a part-time job my  twins are in JK ready to learn my  daughter graduated from high school and  is going to university on a scholarship  we are who we are with and where we are  with them and community remains the  answer Thanks  [Applause]

